1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,120
Take one kilogram. A bag of sugar.

2
00:00:03,420 --> 00:00:06,940
Now push it to ninety-nine per
cent of the speed of light.

3
00:00:07,652 --> 00:00:11,232
The energy you'd need is about
a hundred and thirty megatons.

4
00:00:11,632 --> 00:00:14,852
More than twice the largest
bomb ever detonated.

5
00:00:15,729 --> 00:00:17,409
For one kilogram.

6
00:00:17,789 --> 00:00:19,959
A spacecraft weighs tonnes.

7
00:00:20,249 --> 00:00:22,429
Push a single tonne to
that speed, and you'd use

8
00:00:22,659 --> 00:00:25,469
nearly a year of all the
energy humanity consumes.

9
00:00:25,982 --> 00:00:28,442
And ninety-nine per cent isn't the limit.

10
00:00:29,222 --> 00:00:33,462
Go to ninety-nine point nine, and the
bill is three and a half times higher.

11
00:00:34,082 --> 00:00:36,702
Every extra nine costs more than the last.

12
00:00:37,410 --> 00:00:42,930
This isn't a guess. At the Large Hadron
Collider, protons are pushed so hard that

13
00:00:43,160 --> 00:00:46,530
they fall short of light by only
about three metres per second.

14
00:00:46,751 --> 00:00:48,151
Roughly the speed of a jogger.

15
00:00:48,381 --> 00:00:52,591
And no amount of extra energy
will close that last gap.

16
00:00:52,991 --> 00:00:56,951
The closer you get, the more it
costs, without any limit at all.

17
00:00:57,539 --> 00:00:59,679
So a bigger engine won't do it.

18
00:01:00,259 --> 00:01:04,479
The equations of relativity don't give
a ship a way past the speed of light.

19
00:01:04,967 --> 00:01:09,407
And we'd need one. The nearest star beyond
the Sun is more than four light-years

20
00:01:09,487 --> 00:01:13,027
away, so even light takes
four years to get there.

21
00:01:13,707 --> 00:01:17,707
Crossing our own galaxy takes light
more than a hundred thousand years.

22
00:01:18,379 --> 00:01:22,999
Our actual spacecraft are far slower,
as we saw in an earlier video.

23
00:01:23,510 --> 00:01:26,990
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

24
00:01:27,610 --> 00:01:29,230
It really helps the channel.

25
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:33,200
Relativity does offer one consolation.

26
00:01:34,260 --> 00:01:38,360
At ninety-nine per cent of light
speed, time on board runs slower.

27
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:43,200
The crew would reach the nearest star
in about seven months of their own time.

28
00:01:43,507 --> 00:01:46,347
But back on Earth, more
than four years would pass.

29
00:01:47,087 --> 00:01:50,627
Cross the galaxy that way, and
by the time you arrived, more

30
00:01:50,857 --> 00:01:53,627
than a hundred thousand years
would have passed back home.

31
00:01:54,629 --> 00:01:56,289
So here is the question.

32
00:01:56,989 --> 00:01:59,829
What would we have to invent
to travel faster than light?

33
00:02:00,309 --> 00:02:03,369
The answer, it turns out,
probably isn't an engine.

34
00:02:05,079 --> 00:02:08,359
Let's start with something odd
that the universe already does.

35
00:02:09,039 --> 00:02:10,819
Space itself can stretch.

36
00:02:11,555 --> 00:02:15,175
The universe is expanding, and
galaxies more than about fourteen

37
00:02:15,405 --> 00:02:19,235
billion light-years away are moving
away from us faster than light.

38
00:02:19,865 --> 00:02:21,645
We can still see some of them.

39
00:02:22,165 --> 00:02:24,265
Nothing is breaking the speed limit.

40
00:02:24,805 --> 00:02:27,305
Those galaxies aren't
racing through space.

41
00:02:28,025 --> 00:02:29,865
The space between us is growing.

42
00:02:30,676 --> 00:02:34,736
So what if a ship didn't move
through space, but let space move it?

43
00:02:35,873 --> 00:02:39,193
In 1994, a young Mexican physicist, Miguel

44
00:02:39,423 --> 00:02:42,493
Alcubierre, was working
on his doctorate in Wales.

45
00:02:43,673 --> 00:02:46,133
He found a solution to
Einstein's equations of gravity

46
00:02:46,363 --> 00:02:49,993
that looked a lot like the
warp drive of science fiction.

47
00:02:50,734 --> 00:02:52,794
In front of the ship, space contracts.

48
00:02:53,634 --> 00:02:55,454
Behind it, space expands.

49
00:02:56,114 --> 00:02:58,474
The ship sits in a calm
bubble in the middle.

50
00:02:59,069 --> 00:03:02,109
Inside the bubble, the ship
isn't moving fast at all.

51
00:03:02,749 --> 00:03:04,849
Nobody on board would feel a thing.

52
00:03:04,929 --> 00:03:09,449
But the bubble itself could cross the
distance to a star faster than light.

53
00:03:09,813 --> 00:03:12,573
Instead of driving
faster, you move the road.

54
00:03:13,879 --> 00:03:16,739
But a solution to an
equation is not a machine.

55
00:03:17,439 --> 00:03:19,999
It tells you what space
would have to look like.

56
00:03:20,339 --> 00:03:23,119
It doesn't tell you how
to make it look that way.

57
00:03:23,529 --> 00:03:28,129
And to make it look that way, Alcubierre's
bubble needs something very strange.

58
00:03:28,729 --> 00:03:29,989
Negative energy.

59
00:03:30,431 --> 00:03:32,511
Less energy than empty space.

60
00:03:32,711 --> 00:03:34,311
Less than nothing.

61
00:03:35,609 --> 00:03:38,149
Quantum physics does allow a little of it.

62
00:03:38,629 --> 00:03:40,849
Put two metal plates a tiny fraction of a

63
00:03:41,079 --> 00:03:44,029
millimetre apart, and they
pull towards each other.

64
00:03:44,603 --> 00:03:47,503
Physicists explain this force
by saying the gap between the

65
00:03:47,733 --> 00:03:51,163
plates holds slightly less energy
than the empty space outside.

66
00:03:51,943 --> 00:03:57,203
It was predicted in 1948 and
measured precisely in 1997.

67
00:03:57,941 --> 00:04:00,021
But the amounts are minuscule.

68
00:04:00,161 --> 00:04:04,481
Take plates a metre across, a
thousandth of a millimetre apart.

69
00:04:05,021 --> 00:04:08,461
They pull on each other with the
weight of about a tenth of a gram.

70
00:04:08,877 --> 00:04:13,597
And the energy missing from the gap is
equal to the mass of about three protons.

71
00:04:14,599 --> 00:04:17,179
Quantum theory also seems to limit it.

72
00:04:17,839 --> 00:04:20,119
The more negative the
energy, the smaller the

73
00:04:20,349 --> 00:04:22,979
region it can fill, and
the shorter it can last.

74
00:04:23,521 --> 00:04:25,761
And Alcubierre's bubble needs a lot.

75
00:04:26,861 --> 00:04:31,741
In 1997, two physicists worked out
the minimum for his original design,

76
00:04:32,001 --> 00:04:35,921
with a bubble two hundred metres
wide, moving at the speed of light.

77
00:04:36,611 --> 00:04:39,891
Under their assumptions, the
negative energy came out at about

78
00:04:40,121 --> 00:04:43,811
ten billion times the mass of
the entire visible universe.

79
00:04:45,661 --> 00:04:48,521
Later designs brought that
number down enormously.

80
00:04:49,421 --> 00:04:52,341
A few even avoid negative
energy altogether.

81
00:04:53,021 --> 00:04:55,001
But so far, none of those has been

82
00:04:55,231 --> 00:04:57,821
convincingly shown to
go faster than light.

83
00:04:58,697 --> 00:05:00,177
And there's another catch.

84
00:05:00,997 --> 00:05:04,177
The front edge of the bubble is
out of reach for anyone inside.

85
00:05:04,477 --> 00:05:07,837
So the crew couldn't switch
the bubble on, or steer it.

86
00:05:08,155 --> 00:05:11,035
The road would have to be laid
before the trip ever began.

87
00:05:13,865 --> 00:05:15,865
So maybe we shouldn't move the road.

88
00:05:16,405 --> 00:05:18,045
Maybe we should skip it.

89
00:05:19,213 --> 00:05:23,793
In 1985, Carl Sagan was
finishing a novel called Contact.

90
00:05:24,793 --> 00:05:26,240
His heroine had to cross twenty-five

91
00:05:26,470 --> 00:05:29,373
light-years to the star
Vega, and come back.

92
00:05:29,957 --> 00:05:32,957
In his draft, she flew into a
black hole and came out near Vega.

93
00:05:33,187 --> 00:05:38,397
Sagan asked a friend, the physicist
Kip Thorne, to check the physics.

94
00:05:39,504 --> 00:05:42,204
Thorne's answer was that a
black hole wouldn't work.

95
00:05:42,884 --> 00:05:45,224
Anyone who fell in would never come out.

96
00:05:45,804 --> 00:05:48,444
But there was another shape
in Einstein's equations.

97
00:05:49,576 --> 00:05:52,076
Imagine two points on a sheet of paper.

98
00:05:52,836 --> 00:05:55,376
The shortest way between
them is a straight line.

99
00:05:56,053 --> 00:06:00,853
Now fold the paper, so the two
points touch. Nothing moved faster.

100
00:06:01,513 --> 00:06:02,893
The geometry changed.

101
00:06:03,862 --> 00:06:09,002
That's a wormhole. A tunnel through
spacetime, connecting two distant places.

102
00:06:09,933 --> 00:06:13,453
Wormholes appear in some
solutions to Einstein's equations.

103
00:06:13,633 --> 00:06:18,133
But the simplest ones collapse faster
than anything could get through.

104
00:06:18,714 --> 00:06:22,674
So Thorne and his student, Michael
Morris, asked a different question.

105
00:06:23,174 --> 00:06:25,454
Forget whether wormholes exist.

106
00:06:26,154 --> 00:06:29,594
What would it take to keep one
open, wide enough for a person?

107
00:06:30,185 --> 00:06:32,605
Their paper came out in 1988.

108
00:06:32,845 --> 00:06:36,985
A serious physics paper, which
started with a question about a novel.

109
00:06:37,575 --> 00:06:40,745
The novel later became a
film, with Jodie Foster.

110
00:06:41,647 --> 00:06:43,387
And the answer looked familiar.

111
00:06:44,207 --> 00:06:47,787
The throat of the tunnel would have
to be held open by exotic matter.

112
00:06:48,527 --> 00:06:52,287
Negative energy again, like the
gap between those metal plates.

113
00:06:52,775 --> 00:06:54,695
But not three protons' worth.

114
00:06:55,355 --> 00:06:58,535
In the simplest models, a
throat just two metres wide

115
00:06:58,765 --> 00:07:01,655
needs negative mass roughly
comparable to Jupiter.

116
00:07:02,894 --> 00:07:04,534
Cleverer shapes need far less.

117
00:07:04,694 --> 00:07:08,094
But every version anyone
has found still needs some.

118
00:07:08,629 --> 00:07:10,909
In the novel, Sagan got around that.

119
00:07:11,609 --> 00:07:15,369
The tunnels were built by an ancient
civilisation, long since gone.

120
00:07:15,989 --> 00:07:17,429
We don't have that option.

121
00:07:18,159 --> 00:07:21,199
And no traversable wormhole
has ever been observed.

122
00:07:21,879 --> 00:07:25,439
We don't know if one could exist,
let alone how to build one.

123
00:07:27,989 --> 00:07:32,109
So, what would we actually have
to invent? Three things, at least.

124
00:07:32,823 --> 00:07:35,563
First, a way to reshape spacetime itself.

125
00:07:36,323 --> 00:07:40,323
Not to move through it faster, but
to bend it, on purpose and on demand.

126
00:07:41,049 --> 00:07:43,069
Second, the energy to do it.

127
00:07:43,789 --> 00:07:46,829
For the original warp drive
and for wormholes, that means

128
00:07:47,059 --> 00:07:50,589
energy of a kind we can barely
make in a laboratory, in amounts

129
00:07:50,819 --> 00:07:54,029
that, in some estimates, dwarf
everything in the universe.

130
00:07:54,736 --> 00:07:57,056
And third, a reason to think it's allowed.

131
00:07:57,796 --> 00:08:01,256
Relativity has passed every
test we've ever given it.

132
00:08:01,567 --> 00:08:04,827
The clocks on GPS satellites
run fast by about thirty-eight

133
00:08:04,907 --> 00:08:09,107
millionths of a second a day,
exactly as relativity predicts.

134
00:08:09,767 --> 00:08:12,047
Without correcting for that, GPS

135
00:08:12,277 --> 00:08:14,847
navigation would quickly
become unreliable.

136
00:08:15,788 --> 00:08:18,948
That same theory is the one
that sets the speed limit.

137
00:08:19,088 --> 00:08:22,188
And nothing in it hands
us a way around it.

138
00:08:22,713 --> 00:08:26,433
So faster-than-light travel doesn't
look like an engineering problem.

139
00:08:27,013 --> 00:08:28,733
It looks like a physics problem.

140
00:08:31,433 --> 00:08:34,673
We have two ideas, the warp
drive and the wormhole.

141
00:08:35,113 --> 00:08:36,733
Neither one is a blueprint.

142
00:08:36,913 --> 00:08:38,933
We don't know if either can be built.

143
00:08:40,173 --> 00:08:44,933
But there's a problem even deeper than
energy, or exotic matter, or engineering.

144
00:08:46,152 --> 00:08:50,792
In that same year, 1988, Thorne and
two colleagues showed that a wormhole

145
00:08:51,022 --> 00:08:55,092
built for travel could, in principle,
be turned into a time machine.

146
00:08:55,499 --> 00:08:58,759
And if faster-than-light signals
obey the usual rules of special

147
00:08:58,989 --> 00:09:02,979
relativity, they can be arranged
to arrive before they were sent.

148
00:09:05,679 --> 00:09:09,719
So what if the universe doesn't just
make faster-than-light travel difficult?

149
00:09:12,189 --> 00:09:15,789
What if it makes it incompatible
with cause and effect itself?

150
00:09:19,149 --> 00:09:20,659
That's where we're going next.
